    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tanguts"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanguts.jpg" alt="Tanguts" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%A5%BF%E5%A4%8F%E7%94%B7%E4%BE%9B%E9%A4%8A%E4%BA%BA%E5%83%8F.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a>, Dangxiang to the Chinese and Minyak to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>, came from the highlands of the Qinghai-Sichuan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> region and were held to descend from the Western Qiang. Moving east in the 650s under Tibetan pressure, they were settled by the Tang in 32 jimi prefectures under their own chieftains, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> of the 750s they were the chief power of the Ordos. In 873 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> Sigong seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>; for helping drive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> he received in 881 the four prefectures of the Dingnan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">Jiedushi</a> and in 883 the imperial surname Li and the title Duke of Xia, a frontier command his house held through the Five Dynasties.</p><p>In 982 Li Jipeng, whose succession the elders had not approved, surrendered the command to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. His kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiqian">Li Jiqian</a> refused to submit: he took Yinzhou in 985, paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> from 986, married into Khitan nobility in 989 and in 990 accepted from the Liao emperor the title King of the Xia State, defying and placating the Song by turns until he died of an arrow wound on 6 January 1004. His son Deming sent tribute to both empires and expanded west: Deming's son Yuanhao conquered the Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom in 1028 and the Guiyi Circuit surrendered in 1030. Succeeding his father in 1032, Yuanhao took the Tangut clan name Weiming, levied every able-bodied man between 15 and 60, promulgated the Tangut script in 1036 and in 1038 declared himself emperor of the Great Xia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinchuan">Xingqing</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/movable-type"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/movable-type.jpg" alt="Movable type" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">Movable type</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metal_movable_type.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Willi Heidelbach, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At its height Western Xia ruled about a million square kilometres of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its heart was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, a stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and the chief route between northern China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; agriculture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> and trade with Central Asia were the economy's foundations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> cut off border trade in 1038, and the Jin shut the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a> and Baoan from 1172 to 1197, accusing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a> of trading worthless gems for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. The peace of 1044 brought a Song tribute of 250,000 units of silk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> a year until 1096.</p><p>Tanguts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> served as officials and intermarried freely, and Tangut, Chinese and Tibetan were all official languages. The Tangut script, attributed to Yeli Renrong and based on Chinese and Khitan, was used from 1036 to translate and print Chinese and Tibetan works, and Western Xia pioneered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng">Bi Sheng</a> (972–1051): the Auspicious Tantra of All-Reaching Union is the earliest surviving wooden-type book. Society was patriarchal under the Tangut Code: a woman was the property of her parents, husband and in-laws, could marry at 13 for a bride price and be divorced for the 'Seven Defects', from childlessness to talkativeness; a wife who killed her husband was beheaded, and female slaves counted as livestock. In 1034 Yuanhao decreed that his subjects shave their heads, leaving a fringe at the forehead and temples.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Western Xia was a militarised kingdom. Jingzong's eighteen military commissions in five zones put as many as 370,000 mounted men under arms. Its elite were the Iron Hawks, 3,000 heavy horse, and the Trekker mountain infantry, though the prince Chage complained that the Trekkers were scattered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>'s Mighty-Arm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> and that Xia troops 'can only fight according to convention'.</p><p>Against the Song the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a> won battles and lost wars. In 1039–40 Jingzong besieged Yanzhou with over 100,000 men and annihilated Ren Fu's 30,000 at Haoshuichuan, yet took no territory from forts held by 200,000 rotating Song troops; half his soldiers died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> invasion of 100,000 in 1044 was brutally mauled. In 1081 five Song armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a>, Xiazhou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>, but on 21 December the Xia breached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> dikes and flooded the besiegers of Lingzhou, and in 1082 a 300,000-strong Xia army took Yongle, killing 17,300. The Song 'advance and fortify' campaign of 1097–99, 40 forts across the Ordos, ended Tangut superiority in the field: 100,000 men were routed at Pingxia in 1098. In 1115–19 Tong Guan's invasions ended with Liu Fa's 200,000 surrounded and halved by Chage before the capital and a Song apology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> raided in 1205 and 1207, besieged Zhongxing and its 150,000 defenders in 1209 until his own dike burst, and in 1225–27 brought some 180,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> to defeat Emperor Mo's 300,000 and slaughter the capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jingzong built a Chinese-style government for a Tangut state: a Secretariat, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bureau-of-military-affairs">Bureau of Military Affairs</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorate">Censorate</a> and sixteen bureaus under a chancellor. Succession never settled. When he died on 19 January 1048, stabbed by his disinherited heir, a council of elders chose his cousin until Mocang Epang pressed the claim of his two-year-old nephew Liangzuo (Yizong); the Mocang regents ruled until Yizong and Lady Liang murdered Epang and his clan in 1061. Two generations of the Liang clan followed, empress dowagers and their brothers Liang Yimai and Liang Qipu ruling for the child emperors Huizong (seven in 1068) and Chongzong (three in 1086). The court swung between Chinese and Tangut rites, and Huizong was imprisoned by Liang Yimai from 1081 to 1083.</p><p>Renzong (1139–1193) remade the state on Confucian lines: schools throughout the country, Confucian temples, imperial examinations in 1147, an Inner Academy in 1148 and in 1161 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a> to compile the Xia records. He crushed the general Ren Dejing when Ren demanded half the realm in 1170, and made the Confucian Wo Daochong, translator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> into Tangut, chief minister. The state religion blended Tibetan Tantric and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>; the emperor posed as a Buddhist king and patron of lamas, and from the mid-12th century Tibetan monks held the office of state preceptor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bi-sheng.jpg" alt="Bi Sheng" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng">Bi Sheng</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beijing.Musee_imprimerie.caracteres_mobiles.Bisheng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Popolon, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Western Xia made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> a state. For two centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a> held the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and the Jin, dealing with each as a fourth power: a royal title from the Liao in 990, a Song tribute after 1044, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to the Jin in 1124 and a brief occupation of Jin and Song land in 1161–62. It was against Western Xia that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> first turned his united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, in 1205, 1207 and 1209, and the Xia submission of 1210 opened the Mongols' 23-year war on the Jin.</p><p>The Tanguts' lasting work was in letters and faith. Their script, created by 1036, wrote a Tibeto-Burman language in characters of its own and carried a literature now surviving only in fragments; their printers were the first after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng">Bi Sheng</a> to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>, in clay and in wood, and their Buddhist canon, mostly completed under Renzong, was printed in more than 3,620 juan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> in 1302 under the Mongols for the monasteries of the old Tangut lands. Their art, music and architecture were called 'shining and sparkling', and Khara-Khoto remains a major archaeological site. So complete was the Mongol destruction, and the Yuan compiled no official history of Western Xia as they did for Liao, Jin and Song, that the state's origins stayed obscure until the scholarship of the 20th century. Its remnants scattered to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, where they persisted into the middle of the Ming.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongols.jpg" alt="Mongols" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_mongols_%26_chinois_(1211).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end came from the steppe. In 1203 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a> and used the flight of Toghrul's son through Tangut land as a pretext to raid Western Xia in 1205; in 1206, the year he became Genghis Khan, Emperor Huanzong was deposed by his cousin Anquan (Xiangzong). Genghis raided again in 1207, sacking Wulahai, and in 1209 besieged Zhongxing until his diversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> burst and flooded his own camp. In 1210 Xiangzong submitted, gave Genghis his daughter Chaka and paid tribute, and turned the Xia armies on the Jin. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> demanded troops for their western campaigns in 1217–18 the Xia refused, and Asha Gambu led a court set against both the Mongol alliance and the Jin war.</p><p>Genghis, back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, came to punish them. In 1225 he attacked with some 180,000 men; Asha Gambu mocked his offer of submission and challenged him to battle, and the Mongols destroyed city after city, Khara-Khoto in early 1226 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Liangzhou</a> in August, and in November besieged Lingwu as Emperor Xianzong died. His successor Mo led 300,000 men to defeat and Lingzhou was sacked. The siege of the capital lasted six months into 1227. Genghis died in August 1227, his death kept secret; in September Emperor Mo surrendered and was executed, the city's people were slaughtered, the imperial tombs plundered and the state annihilated. John Man has called it perhaps the first recorded attempt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>: the Tangut language and script are extinct.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Western Xia, the Tangut 'Great State of White and High', ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> and the Ordos from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a>'s proclamation in 1038, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> to a tributary peace, survived as a fourth power beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and Jin, and built a Buddhist, Confucian-schooled state with its own script and movable-type <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s armies destroyed it utterly in 1227.</em></p>

